We will go with t3.micro 
Step 1: Check Linux distribution
cat /etc/os-release

Step 2: Check system architecture
uname -m

Possible output:
x86_64 or aarch64

This tells us whether your EC2 instance uses an x86-64 or ARM-based processor.
Step 3: Check CPU
nproc

This displays the number of available processing units.
Step 4: Check RAM
free -h

Step 5: Check disk space
df -h

Step 6: Check AWS CLI
sudo snap install aws-cli --classic
aws --version

We will need AWS CLI later to verify that Terraform successfully creates AWS resources.

Practical 2 – Install Terraform
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

Install the required packages:
sudo apt install -y gnupg wget

Download and register the HashiCorp signing key:
wget -O- https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg

Add the official repository:
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(grep -oP '(?<=UBUNTU_CODENAME=).*' /etc/os-release || lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list

Update package information:
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

Install Terraform:
sudo apt install terraform -y

Verify installation:
terraform version

Practical 3 – Your First Terraform Program
Step 1: Create a project directory
mkdir terraform-practicals
Move inside:
cd terraform-practicals

Create your first file:
nano main.tf

resource "local_file" "hello" {

  filename = "hello.txt"

  content = "Hello! I am learning Terraform."

}

Step 4: Initialize Terraform
terraform init

Step 5: Validate configuration
terraform validate

Step 6: Create an execution plan
terraform plan

Step 7: Apply configuration
terraform apply

Step 8: Verify
ls

You should see:
main.tf
hello.txt

Read the file:
cat hello.txt

Step 9: Destroy the resource
terraform destroy
ls
